is focused on this relationship just  maximizing cover crop biomass whereas  we've shifted the focus over here to par  transmittance or shading and what we  found both in the low end high-density  treatments as well as all the other  treatments I couldn't discuss was that  in fact shading was a much better  predictor of common ragweed so you can  see here we definitely had higher  r-squared values when we look at this  relationship and what's interesting to  note is as we saw with the low density  in that high or that over yielding and  that lower density treatment high  biomass production doesn't necessarily  result in greater shading so it  definitely suggests to us that we really  need to focus in on shading to really  tackle this issue of early emerging  weeds like common ragweed in terms of  soybean yield we actually saw no  differences between seeding ratio or  management treatments which was  surprising given the differences in  cover crop biomass as well as weed  biomass and on average we got about 40  bushels per acre which is actually the  lowest yields we had across all of our  different  and our on-farm sites we typically get  45 to 50 bushels which is really  competitive with other organically  produced soybeans so tillage based  systems when we compare this to the  intro cultivation we see about 80% of  that yield and so that's definitely a  substantial yield loss there again this  is a little bit related to these were  not very productive treatments for us  and of course you need to factor in the  fact that there's going to be a 30  percent reduction in diesel fuel and  labor with this type of management so in  summary we found that early season  shading definitely was an important  predictor  we're coming driver of common ragweed  biomass but these mixtures of barley and  cereal rye might not be the best way to  actually achieve that enhanced shading  we're definitely encouraged to continue  pursuing this because pre-empting that  common ragweed biomass is just so  critical for addressing these issues for  farmers in the Northeast I also found  that high residue cultivation was really  useful for controlling that inter-row  common ragweed and you can think of it  as a rescue cultivation and for long  term weed seed bank management this  might be a really useful tool and then  this is actually different than what  we'd expected soybean yield was not  higher when you integrated that high  residue cultivation that's in some ways  this might be a desirable result for us  because what it suggests is if we focus  on cover crop management and so we can  really suppress those weeds with just  standard no-till you're not gonna see a  yield loss if you don't integrate high  residue cultivation and really for  farmers they're not that interested when  we've gone to grow armies and talked  about this in using high residue  cultivator because the whole purpose or  their whole interest in this is a  reduced tillage system so this idea of  higher education is is it's not very  popular and we definitely see a high  potential profitability for this system  like I said if you get these competitive  yields in combination with this  reduction in energy use this is going to  translate into higher profits for  farmers and so with that I just like to  thank particularly Chris Pelzer Brian  Caldwell and the rest of our lab and all  the folks at the Musgrave Research Forum  I certainly could have done this Reece  which without everyone's assistance and  support so there's time I'd love to take  some questions thank you this has been a  production of Cornell University on the  web at cornell edu


